<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oda clan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oda were a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> clan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a> who served as deputy governors under the Shiba before becoming independent warlords in the 16th century. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> they defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imagawa-yoshimoto">Imagawa Yoshimoto</a> at Okehazama in 1560, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> in 1568 and destroyed the Takeda in 1582, uniting much of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. After Nobunaga's death in 1582 power passed to his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oda-clan.jpg" alt="Oda clan" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gilyellows · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oda_emblem.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> first claimed that the family descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujiwara-clan">Fujiwara clan</a> and later from Taira no Sukemori of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-clan">Taira clan</a>. According to the clan's official genealogy, after Sukemori was killed at the Battle of Dannoura in 1185, his son Taira no Chikazane was entrusted to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> priest at a shrine in Otanosho in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/echizen-province">Echizen Province</a> and became the founder of the Oda.</p><p>Modern scholars find no evidence for the Taira descent. One theory holds that the family came from the Inbe clan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shinto">Shinto</a> priests in Otanosho. Another suggests that Nobunaga claimed Taira ancestry to justify his seizure of power, drawing on a belief of the time that the Minamoto and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-clan">Taira clans</a> were destined to alternate in rule, though it has been questioned whether people then really held this belief.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the middle of the Muromachi period the Oda served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiba-clan">Shiba clan</a>, military governors of Echizen. When Shiba Yoshishige was appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owari-province">Owari Province</a> the Oda followed him there, and Oda Jōshō became deputy governor. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Ōnin War</a> the Shiba split into two warring factions, and from around 1466 the Oda did the same, with Iwakura Castle and Kiyosu Castle as the strongholds of the two sides.</p><p>As the Shiba declined, Oda Tatsusada rebelled against them in 1513, failed and was killed. Oda Nobutomo then made the Shiba his puppets. During the Tenbun period Oda Nobuhide overtook the main family, captured Nagoya Castle in 1538 and became the strongest lord in Owari. Oda Nobunaga, operating from Nagoya Castle, besieged and killed Nobutomo, his uncle, in 1555, and his branch then brought the whole province under its control before turning on the Imagawa, Saitō, Azai, Asakura and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-clan">Takeda clans</a>. Nobunaga was forced to suicide by his vassal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akechi-mitsuhide">Akechi Mitsuhide</a> in the Incident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honno-ji">Honnō-ji</a> in 1582.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Oda did not disappear after Nobunaga. One branch became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hatamoto">hatamoto</a>, direct retainers of the Tokugawa shogun, and others ruled as minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>. At the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-period">Edo period</a> these were the domains of Tendō in Dewa Province and Kaibara in Tanba Province, each rated at 20,000 koku, and Yanagimoto and Kaiju in Yamato Province, each at 10,000 koku. Under the daimyo Oda Nobutoshi the Oda of Tendō signed the pact that created the Ōuetsu Reppan Dōmei.</p><p>When the feudal domains were abolished in 1871, after the Meiji Restoration, the heads of all four houses were made viscounts in the new hereditary peerage. Descendants of the clan live throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> today, mainly in the south and south-west.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oda clan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son Katsuyori lost much of his army to <b>Oda</b> and Tokugawa musketeers at Nagashino in 1575; in 1582 their forces invaded, vassals defected, and Katsuyori killed himself at Tenmokuzan.</blockquote><a href="/state/takeda-clan-domain">Takeda clan domain</a></li><li><blockquote>He defeated an <b>Oda</b> army at Tedorigawa in 1577.</blockquote><a href="/state/uesugi-clan-domain">Uesugi clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
